 q="윗글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?",
 passage="In the 1950s, engineers designing cockpits for the U.S. Air Force measured thousands of pilots and built seats to fit the 'average' pilot. Accidents remained high. When one researcher checked how many pilots actually fell within the average range on ten key body dimensions, the answer was zero. A cockpit designed for the average fit no one. The solution was adjustable seats, pedals, and helmet straps — features we now take for granted in every car. The lesson extends beyond aviation: systems designed around an imaginary average person often fail the real individuals they are meant to serve.",
